WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.600
Hi there.

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:01.920
This is Steve Kaufman.

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:05.040
It's uh, just about New
Year's Eve here in Vancouver.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.090
I'm gonna talk today about
my New Year's resolutions for

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.800
resolutions for the coming year.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.430
By the time you see the video,
we're probably in the new year.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.279
Remember, if you enjoy my videos,
please subscribe, click on the bell for

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.230
notifications, and if you follow me on a
podcast service, please leave a comment.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.160
I do appreciate it.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:28.850
So today I'm gonna talk about what
I intend to do as regards, you know,

00:00:29.185 --> 00:00:30.735
language earning in the coming year.

00:00:31.005 --> 00:00:34.185
But first, let me explain
why I have my vyshyvanka on.

00:00:34.785 --> 00:00:39.375
So I was getting ready to, uh, do my
New Year's video, and I happened to

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:45.585
check my Twitter feed, and I see that
Ukraine is once again being bombed.

00:00:45.705 --> 00:00:53.925
The whole country is facing this evil,
uh, I can only call it genocidal attack

00:00:53.925 --> 00:00:59.385
from the Putin regime  and I just, it
just bothers me so much because it's

00:00:59.385 --> 00:01:07.965
so unprovoked, unjustified, just the
random evil destruction of cities, of

00:01:07.965 --> 00:01:10.815
civil infrastructure, killing people.

00:01:11.205 --> 00:01:15.884
Uh, and of course they have their, uh,
occupation troops there, shelling towns.

00:01:15.884 --> 00:01:19.815
For example, the town of
Krasnohorivka, which I visited,

00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:22.335
uh, has been under constant attack.

00:01:22.335 --> 00:01:25.365
I think it's been largely
destroyed, like so much of Ukraine.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.770
I was there some years ago,
visited with the people there.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.920
It's very near the city of
Donetsk, Donetsk, but it's on

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.539
the Ukrainian controlled side.

00:01:35.289 --> 00:01:35.410
Horrible.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.690
I just, I just find it's unbelievable
that in this day and age, we are

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:46.350
tolerating the random slaughter
that Putin has inflicted on Ukraine.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:51.440
And so before I get into the subject of
my language earning goals for the coming

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:58.200
year I just can't help but expressing
sympathy for Ukraine and the wish that

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:06.000
in the new year, the rest of the world is
able to prevent this ongoing massacre and,

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:12.320
and or that Ukraine is able to properly
defeat Russia and get all the Russian

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:18.840
troops out of Ukraine, all of Ukraine, to
respect Ukraine's territorial sovereignty.

00:02:18.869 --> 00:02:23.569
Because if we don't, if the new
world order is that big countries

00:02:23.584 --> 00:02:28.530
can simply beat up on small countries
or powerful countries have the right

00:02:28.535 --> 00:02:33.780
to, uh, set their foreign, uh, policy
agenda but smaller countries don't.

00:02:34.185 --> 00:02:37.605
Uh, big countries have the right to
decide who their neighbors are going

00:02:37.605 --> 00:02:41.505
to be and who their neighbors are
allied with, and small countries don't,

00:02:41.655 --> 00:02:44.415
that's just not a formula for peace.

00:02:44.774 --> 00:02:49.394
So I think the whole world has to
unite behind supporting Ukraine.

00:02:49.605 --> 00:02:53.625
I can't, you know, express strongly
enough how I feel about that subject.

00:02:53.975 --> 00:02:54.864
Enough said.

00:02:55.364 --> 00:02:59.445
Now, my New Year's resolutions when it
comes to language learning, we do have

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:05.880
challenges at LingQ where people are
encouraged to maintain their streak or to,

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.310
you know, undertake a 30-day challenge,
a 90-day challenge, which are sort of

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:16.560
ways of nudging people forward, keep
them on task, give them short term goals.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:21.750
Like I want to get, you know, read so
many words today or save so many words.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:26.250
So all of this is very valuable in getting
people going and keeping them going,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.100
however, you know, I've been at this
learning of languages for a long time, and

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.540
in particular I've been with my Persian
and Arabic and even a little bit of

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.550
Turkish a little more just recently too.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:47.280
And so my New Year's resolution is that
I, I want to follow my own advice and

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:53.190
find ways to enjoy the learning process
as much as possible, so I'm not worrying

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:59.535
about how much improve  in Persian or in
Arabic, in Levantine Arabic or Standard

00:03:59.535 --> 00:04:02.505
Arabic or Egyptian Arabic or in Turkish.

00:04:02.895 --> 00:04:09.975
I just want to enjoy sort of in a
dilettante way, sampling a little

00:04:09.975 --> 00:04:13.815
bit of Persian here, a little
bit of Arabic there and so forth.

00:04:14.805 --> 00:04:18.225
Now I was thinking about my
language learning when I was

00:04:18.225 --> 00:04:19.785
out, uh, cross-country skiing.

00:04:20.204 --> 00:04:23.685
So when you're cross-country skiing,
there are times when you're gliding

00:04:23.685 --> 00:04:26.610
of course, but there are times
when you're going up the hill.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.450
So when you start in a language, you're
very much going up the hill because

00:04:30.455 --> 00:04:34.440
it's, it's hard work, but you're
certainly aware of getting up that hill.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:38.790
And that's, I think, where, you
know, having daily targets is very

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.110
important 'cause it keeps you going.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.250
But when you're in that gliding period,
you've also gotta be enjoying it.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.440
You gotta take time to enjoy the scenery.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:50.430
And if I think back to when I
learned, uh, other languages like

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.595
Russian,  Ukrainian or Italian.

00:04:54.105 --> 00:04:57.105
I had wonderful, you know,
audio books that I listened to.

00:04:57.105 --> 00:05:00.495
I had wonderful material
that was meaningful to me

00:05:00.495 --> 00:05:01.935
that I enjoyed listening to.

00:05:01.935 --> 00:05:06.285
And then I could go back and, and read it
on LingQ and look up words and phrases.

00:05:06.645 --> 00:05:09.345
And I can remember going through
the words, listening to, uh,

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:13.465
you know, uh, ... in Russian.

00:05:13.485 --> 00:05:17.835
You know, uh, Fathers and Sons or
listening, you know, jogging and

00:05:17.835 --> 00:05:18.885
listening to I promessi sposi I.

00:05:19.245 --> 00:05:21.304
Pardon me 'cause I like
19th century literature.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:27.630
Uh, so I'm gonna try to find, I'm gonna
try to find something in Persian, uh,

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:33.360
because the danger, what I find myself
doing now is I keep on going back and

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:37.710
reviewing the old stuff, and I always
discover something new and I feel that

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:42.900
I'm reinforcing things and maybe I'm
improving, but I'm not pushing myself

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:49.590
into new stuff and I haven't found
that, sort of, novel, maybe 19th century

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:54.770
novel in Persian, if such a thing
exists, from the Qajar Dynasty or...

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.380
I'm gonna speak to Sahra.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.210
I'm gonna meet with her tomorrow
morning online and see if we can't find

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:04.380
something that's sort of a, a story or
'cause a lot of what I'm doing is...

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.840
I have her material on
the history of Iran.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.740
I have a number of other things in our
library, but I, sort of, an equivalent

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:15.630
of, uh, you know, a novel or something
that I could listen to and where I

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:21.599
might be able to find the, uh, pdf or
the digital text so that I can work...

00:06:21.659 --> 00:06:24.039
maybe I'm dreaming, maybe
that's not available, but

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:25.560
I'll have to find something.

00:06:25.565 --> 00:06:30.210
I need to get something that pushes
me beyond reviewing the content

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:32.490
that I've already, you know, done.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:36.525
And of course the temptation in
something like Persian and Arabic

00:06:36.525 --> 00:06:39.914
is to stay with easy material
because it's so hard to read for me.

00:06:40.395 --> 00:06:44.054
Uh, and yet when I think back
with with Chinese, which is also

00:06:44.054 --> 00:06:48.224
difficult from a reading point of
view, uh, I did push myself further.

00:06:48.224 --> 00:06:53.804
So my New Year's resolution is not
so much the sort of, uh, meeting

00:06:53.804 --> 00:07:00.414
my daily targets or, um, you
know, uh, maintaining my streak.

00:07:00.414 --> 00:07:04.305
Uh, in a way it would be nice if at
LingQ we gave you credit for maintaining,

00:07:04.305 --> 00:07:07.575
you know, to being active in say,
two or three different languages.

00:07:07.784 --> 00:07:12.344
We only maintain the streak in one
language and maybe the majority of people

00:07:12.344 --> 00:07:18.914
only study one language, so it's perhaps
not necessary to try to have a, a combined

00:07:19.245 --> 00:07:23.805
streak, but it would be helpful for me
'casue whatever I choose to do, even

00:07:23.805 --> 00:07:28.995
now, we've changed this sort of incentive
from one of, uh, having to specifically,

00:07:29.055 --> 00:07:33.255
you know, read so many words and save
so many words, is combined in coins.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.465
So as long as you maintain a certain
level of coins, whether you're

00:07:36.465 --> 00:07:40.005
listening or reading or speaking or
whatever you're doing, uh, you're

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:41.645
getting credit for your activity.

00:07:41.955 --> 00:07:46.365
You're not forced to have, you know,
x amount of activity for, for reading

00:07:46.365 --> 00:07:48.105
and x amount of activity for speaking.

00:07:48.224 --> 00:07:49.034
You can do what you want.

00:07:49.515 --> 00:07:53.864
It would be nice if we could combine
that across three languages, but I don't

00:07:53.864 --> 00:07:57.015
think many people are studying three
languages, so it's difficult for me to

00:07:57.015 --> 00:08:02.325
convince, uh, my son Mark, who runs LingQ,
that we should invest time in coming up

00:08:02.325 --> 00:08:06.705
with a combined, you know, coin number.

00:08:06.705 --> 00:08:08.775
But in any case, that's what I'm gonna do.

00:08:08.865 --> 00:08:15.045
I'm gonna continue sort of dilettante-like
and trying to find ways to enjoy my

00:08:15.045 --> 00:08:20.295
Persian, my Arabic, my Turkish, without
worrying too much about how many words

00:08:20.295 --> 00:08:23.805
I'm saving or reading, uh, without
necessarily worrying about how much

00:08:23.805 --> 00:08:29.655
progress I'm making, but rather making
sure that I enjoy the process and if

00:08:29.655 --> 00:08:32.265
we enjoy the process, we will improve.

00:08:32.295 --> 00:08:33.165
We needn't worry.

00:08:33.855 --> 00:08:39.855
So there you have it, my New Year's
resolution and, uh, I hope the

00:08:39.855 --> 00:08:41.625
New Year brings peace to Ukraine.

00:08:42.405 --> 00:08:46.905
Uh, I hope the New Year brings language
learning enjoyment to all of us.

00:08:46.905 --> 00:08:47.535
All of you.

00:08:47.535 --> 00:08:48.225
All of us.

00:08:48.735 --> 00:08:52.365
And if it brings us enjoyment,
it'll also bring us success.

00:08:52.635 --> 00:08:55.745
Thank you for listening
and all the best for 2023.

00:08:56.375 --> 00:08:57.035
Bye for now.

